🧭 PROJECT: New Jersey Affordability Erosion Platform
Goal

Build a high-confidence, time-series affordability system for New Jersey counties that measures:

Housing burden over time
True tax-adjusted disposable income
Required income to afford median housing
“Affordability erosion” (how far middle-class purchasing power has degraded)

The priority is accuracy over coverage. No speculative shortcuts.

1. Core Design Philosophy (non-negotiable)
1.1 Data quality > feature breadth
Prefer fewer geographies with clean, consistent time series
Avoid ZIP-level noise early (start at county level)
1.2 Temporal consistency matters more than granularity
A stable 1990–2026 series is more valuable than high-resolution but broken data
1.3 Every metric must be reproducible
Every value should be derivable from stored raw inputs + deterministic transforms
2. Geographic Scope (Phase 1 only)

Start with:

New Jersey counties (21 total)
Focus first on:
Somerset
Middlesex
Bergen
Hudson
Essex
Union
Morris

Then expand statewide.

3. Data Sources (strict selection rules)
3.1 Income (must be stable + comparable over time)

Primary:

U.S. Census ACS 5-year estimates
Decennial Census (anchor points pre-2005)

Rules:

Never mix single-year ACS with 5-year without labeling
Always store margin of error where available
3.2 Housing (must be index-based, not raw listings)

Preferred:

Zillow Home Value Index (ZHVI) Zillow
Redfin county-level price series Redfin

Rules:

Do NOT use scraped listings (too noisy)
Always normalize to annual median index
3.3 Taxes (most important and most error-prone layer)

Federal:

IRS brackets per year Internal Revenue Service

State (NJ-specific):

New Jersey Division of Taxation tables

Property tax:

NJ Department of Community Affairs (county averages)
MUST be modeled separately per county

Rules:

Effective tax rate must be simulated, not assumed flat
Store full tax function per year (not just %)
3.4 Inflation adjustment (required for time comparability)
CPI-U (BLS)

All values must be converted to:

2026 real dollars baseline

