6. Pipeline Architecture
Step 1: Raw ingestion layer
Pull ACS income data
Pull ZHVI / Redfin indices
Pull IRS + NJ tax tables

Store raw untouched tables.

Step 2: Normalization layer
Align all datasets by:
county_id
year
Convert all dollars → 2026 CPI-adjusted
Step 3: Simulation layer
Compute:
taxes by income band
mortgage payments per year
property tax effects

This is where most systems fail—keep deterministic and versioned.

Step 4: Derived metrics layer
compute burden ratios
compute disposable income
compute required income
Step 5: Serving layer
API endpoints:
/county/{id}/trend
/state/nj/map
/compare?countyA=&countyB=
7. Visualization requirements (v1)
Must-have views
7.1 Time series chart (core insight)
Burden ratio (1990–2026)
Required income vs actual income
7.2 NJ county map
Color = affordability gap index
7.3 “Collapse curve”

Single chart per county:

Income line
Required income line
Divergence area shaded

This is your “viral insight chart”

8. Data validation rules (critical)

You must enforce:

8.1 Cross-source validation
Census income vs BLS wage consistency check
8.2 Outlier detection
Year-over-year jumps >15% flagged
8.3 Missing data handling
Never interpolate blindly
Mark gaps explicitly
8.4 Versioning
Every metric computation should be reproducible by:
dataset version
formula version
